WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.259
Film Courage: When you’re attempting to
sell a screenplay, what are some of the deals

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:05.259
you will encounter?

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:06.259
&nbsp;

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:09.760
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: &nbsp;Well
there is the option agreement which is to

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.300
shop the material so they are not buying the
script.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.460
There’s provisions for purchase of it but
it’s an option which means they have the

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:24.680
rights for let’s say three to six months
and they can have you do work on it or possibly

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:33.430
somebody else but they are optioning the rights
and you have to be…I’d say careful but

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:40.020
it’s going to take if off the market obviously
because they are paying you.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.551
My first script sale (the spec) was optioned
three times in a row and I was doing all of

00:00:45.551 --> 00:00:50.989
the rewrites which is good so they optioned
it for a number of…for quite a while (six

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:52.890
months at a time).

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.980
This allows them to find financing and that
may fall through so they don’t want to let

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.899
the project go and also have their imprint
on it you know what I mean?

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:06.820
So when it came time to execute the option
that means they have to purchase the script

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:17.340
and that was all in the contract and there
is also the outright purchase of the screenplay

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:24.900
and then you are usually given a chance to
rewrite it one draft with notes (with a polish)

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.820
and then if it doesn’t quite gel then there
is a provision for you to be let go and somebody

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.799
else can be brought on.

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:39.130
And then every time it’s a step deal so
there are steps.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.950
Money to start - so when you read in the “Trades”
that the script sold for a million dollars,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:44.979
they didn’t hand over a million dollars.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:48.960
It’s only if the movie gets made does all
the money get paid.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:56.250
It all sounds great but you get money up front
to start and let’s say you have to have

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:01.579
to do the outline or treatment you get money
for that to start and turn in so there’s

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:02.579
steps.

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:08.290
Then rewrites so the first draft if you’re
doing an assignment it’s the same thing,

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:13.880
money to write the first draft, money when
you turn it in, so it all goes by steps and

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.551
then there are provisions in each step for
how long it takes to read it and also for

00:02:18.551 --> 00:02:21.920
how long you’re given to write it.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.660
I mean after you write it there’s responsibilities
on both sides.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.730
Your responsibility to write it and the producer’s
responsibility to read it in a timely fashion.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:39.830
Meaning that they can’t go away for six
months and then suddenly get back to you.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:43.130
Those are traditionally the type of working
contracts.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:43.730
Question: What else should screenwriters know
about selling a screenplay?

